{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>She has slaughtered</b> — It is the language of slaughter, 'we are reckoned as sheep for slaughter'; and also the language of cooking, 'confections and cooked dishes and baked goods'; and both derive from Arabic: with the point of tet it denotes slaughter, and with the point of chet it denotes cooking." "<b>She has mixed</b> — Like 'she has mingled.' And so: 'mingled wine, mixed wine'; and it is the blending of water with it — and this is its preparation for drinking, like cooking for food."], :book :proverbs, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra cites two biblical phrases as parallels; the second is from 1 Samuel 8:13 (לרקחות ולטבחות ולאופות). The Arabic phonological distinction between ט/ח as a difference of pointing is rendered literally.", 1 "The word מסכה is glossed as equivalent to מזגה, the parallel form from the same root meaning 'to mix/mingle wine with water.'"}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Proverbs -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/mishley/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:21:40.921961448Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}